  "summary": "Defensive circular or square communal housing structures built by Hakka people.",
  "description": "Large, multi-story circular or square walled structures with living quarters surrounding a central courtyard designed for both habitation and defense against hostile elements. Developed by Hakka people as they migrated south, incorporating defensive features while maintaining communal living arrangements.",
  "culturalContext": "Hakka walled villages — beyond Fujian's round tulou, the Guangdong rectangular walled compounds (weilongwu, \"dragon-ring houses\") of Meizhou and Shenzhen — enclosed clan life in fortress geometry: square perimeter walls with corner watchtowers, half-moon ponds, ancestral halls on axis, and layered defensive courtyards. Distinct from tulou's rammed-earth rings, the Guangdong weilong built brick-and-stone; sites like Shenzhen's Dapeng Fortress and Ganxi's Hakka villages anchor the migration-history tourism of the Hakka diaspora.",
